# `-plugin PLUGIN` (a `noop` row of docs/status/options.md: "mortar loads
# no linker plugin; an LTO input is refused by name when it is read";
# spec/input.md "LTO objects"). mortar does not open the plugin: a link
# that names one that does not exist is the same bytes as the link
# without it. What the plugin would have done, link-time optimization
# of a GCC LTO object, is refused by name at the object: a `gcc -flto`
# line (whose driver passes `-plugin liblto_plugin.so` itself) fails with
# `GCC LTO object`, never links without the code. GNU ld loads the plugin
# and links that line, but its map then names the plugin's own
# `/tmp/cc*.ltrans0.ltrans.o`, an input on no command line, which the
# verifier reports (I1); so that link is not made here. GNU ld refuses a
# plugin that does not exist, so the first lines are mortar's alone.
#@ spec: input.md "LTO objects"

cc -O1 prog.c -o prog.o
cc -O1 -flto lto.c -o lto.o
link(mortar) gcc -static prog.o -Wl,-plugin,nosuch-plugin.so -o plugin
link(mortar) gcc -static prog.o -o plain
check(mortar) cmp plugin plain
  has: ^identical$
link(mortar) gcc -static -flto prog.o lto.o -o lto
  exit: fail
  diag: GCC LTO object

#--- prog.c
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) { printf("7\n"); return 0; }
#--- lto.c
int lto_fn(void) { return 7; }
